== a staged-only file has an id, kept through publish
  A staged: x.txt=<id1>
  A published: x.txt=<id1>
== a rename and a rewrite keep it; a peer mints its own
  <id1> resolves to x.txt
  <id1> resolves to y.txt
  A: x.txt=none  y.txt=<id1>
  B: y.txt=<id2>
== a peer's rename keeps the receiver's id
  B: y.txt=none  z.txt=<id2>
== a rename onto a file gives the destination the source's id
  A before: p.txt=<id3>  q.txt=<id4>
  A after: p.txt=none  q.txt=<id3>
== a folder move carries its files' ids, and they resolve there
  <id5> resolves to moved/f.txt
== deletes are noted with the id the file had
  B: z.txt=none
  A's applied log:
    put x.txt                fid=<id1>
    rename x.txt->y.txt      fid=<id1>
    put y.txt                fid=<id1>
    rename y.txt->z.txt      fid=<id1>
    put p.txt                fid=<id3>
    put q.txt                fid=<id4>
    rename p.txt->q.txt      fid=<id3>
    mkdir dir                fid=-
    put dir/f.txt            fid=<id5>
    rename dir->moved        fid=-
    delete z.txt             fid=<id1>
  B's applied log:
    put y.txt                fid=<id2>
    rename y.txt->z.txt      fid=<id2>
    put q.txt                fid=<id6>
    mkdir moved              fid=-
    delete z.txt             fid=<id2>
    put moved/f.txt          fid=<id7>
== a peer's delete under a folder renamed here, rename unpublished
  B's X/f.txt: <id8>
  B: Y/f.txt=none
  last applied: delete Y/f.txt fid=<id8>
== the same with the rename published
  B's X2/f.txt: <id9>
  B: Y2/f.txt=none
  last applied: delete Y2/f.txt fid=<id9>
== owner start gives an id to a file found without one
  B before: q.txt=<id6>
  B without its marker: q.txt=none
  a start after a complete pass: 0
  the first pass: 1
  B after: q.txt=<id10>
  the next one: 0
== cycles started together run one at a time
  feed lines noted: 1 new, noted twice: 0
== a clean stop keeps the index for the next start
  snapshot after the stop: true
  snapshot after the start: false
  <id5> resolves to moved/f.txt
== a marker change after the stop removes the snapshot
  snapshot after the stop: true
  snapshot: false
== without a snapshot the start builds it, with changes made meanwhile
  <id5> resolves to moved/f.txt
  the id minted during the build resolves to during.txt
